WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:15.437
When the Greek king of Thessaly, Peleus, married, he was so enamored of his beautiful wife, it never occurred to him that the gods were meddling in his fate—and that the gods would cause the death of his son, Achilles, on the plains of Troy.

2
00:00:15.438 --> 00:00:24.317
King Peleus' wife was Thetis, an immortal and beautiful sea nymph loved by Zeus, the most powerful of the gods.

3
00:00:24.318 --> 00:00:32.358
Thetis, however, had not returned Zeus' love, so he had sent her to earth to marry a mortal as punishment.

4
00:00:32.359 --> 00:00:37.626
Still, Thetis bore Peleus a son, whom they named Achilles.

5
00:00:37.627 --> 00:00:39.881
The Greek king was overjoyed.

6
00:00:39.882 --> 00:00:50.770
A seer revealed to Thetis that Achilles would be a great war hero, but rather than celebrate the news, Thetis worried that her son would die young in battle.

7
00:00:50.771 --> 00:00:58.064
Desperate to protect him, she carried Achilles away to the underworld, where the River Styx ran deep.

8
00:00:58.065 --> 00:01:09.579
The infernal river had mysterious powers, so Thetis held the boy above the rushing water, grasping him by his heel, and dipped him into it headfirst.

9
00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:19.610
Immersing Achilles in the magical waters would protect him from harm, but Thetis failed to notice that the heel she held did not get wet.

10
00:01:19.611 --> 00:01:25.697
This one spot untouched by the river became the only vulnerable place on his body.

11
00:01:25.706 --> 00:01:33.579
As Achilles was growing up, a war was brewing—its causes extending all the way back to Peleus' wedding.

12
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:43.660
Thetis and Peleus had neglected to invite Eris, the goddess of discord, to their wedding banquet, and she arrived uninvited and incensed.

13
00:01:43.661 --> 00:01:49.589
She threw a golden apple among the guests with a message written on it: "For the fairest."

14
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:58.962
Three goddesses—Hera, Athena, and Aphrodite—all reached for the apple at the same time, and an argument erupted among them.

15
00:01:58.963 --> 00:02:06.145
Zeus suggested that Paris, the young prince of Troy, should choose who was fairest to end the dispute.

16
00:02:06.146 --> 00:02:15.962
The three goddesses all tempted Paris with rewards—Hera with the chance to rule all of Europe and Asia, and Athena with the gift of wisdom.

17
00:02:15.963 --> 00:02:20.748
But the young prince selected Aphrodite, the goddess of love.

18
00:02:20.749 --> 00:02:25.795
Her reward was the love of Helen, the most beautiful woman in the world.

19
00:02:25.796 --> 00:02:32.755
Unfortunately, Helen was already married to the king of the Greek city of Sparta, Menelaus.

20
00:02:32.756 --> 00:02:41.731
True to her word, Aphrodite arranged for Paris to be invited to Menelaus' home, where he was introduced to the gorgeous queen.

21
00:02:41.732 --> 00:02:47.994
The moment the king left them alone, Paris abducted Helen and took her back to Troy.

22
00:02:47.995 --> 00:03:00.569
When Menelaus discovered what Paris had done, he called on his fellow Greek kings and, with his brother, Agamemnon, amassed a vast army that sailed for Troy in a thousand ships.

23
00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:11.015
The Trojan War was the greatest conflict the ancient world had seen, but a seer named Calchas had prophesied its outcome years before it began.

24
00:03:11.016 --> 00:03:17.240
Troy would be taken by the Greeks, but not without the help of the great warrior Achilles.

25
00:03:17.241 --> 00:03:28.176
When Thetis had first heard the seer's prediction, she was alarmed, and she sent her young son away to the court of King Lycomedes on the island of Skyros.

26
00:03:28.177 --> 00:03:40.954
There, Achilles disguised himself as a girl and lived peacefully for many years, but in secret, he taught himself to use a sword and shield and learned hand-to-hand combat.

27
00:03:40.955 --> 00:03:50.208
Agamemnon also knew of Calchas' prophecy, and he sent Odysseus, the cleverest of the Greek kings, to look for Achilles.

28
00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:58.358
When the wily Odysseus arrived at Skyros with his men, he arranged a test to draw Achilles out from among the women.

29
00:03:58.359 --> 00:04:07.407
Odysseus ordered two carts to be set in the city plaza, one filled with fine clothes and perfume, the other with weapons of war.

30
00:04:07.408 --> 00:04:16.608
Achilles' interest in fighting betrayed him; all the women lunged for the clothes in the first cart, but Achilles went to the second.

31
00:04:16.609 --> 00:04:24.370
Odysseus recognized Achilles and convinced him to join the Greek expedition and lead an army against Troy.

32
00:04:24.371 --> 00:04:29.664
When the Greeks arrived at Troy, they discovered the city was heavily fortified.

33
00:04:29.665 --> 00:04:36.751
The armies battled on the plains before the city walls, but the Greeks could not penetrate Troy's defenses.

34
00:04:36.752 --> 00:04:42.457
Frustrated, the Greeks attacked and destroyed 23 towns surrounding Troy.

35
00:04:42.458 --> 00:04:49.711
In one of these, Lyrnessos, Achilles fell in love with a woman named Briseis and took her captive.

36
00:04:49.712 --> 00:04:53.210
But Agamemnon claimed Briseis for himself.

37
00:04:53.211 --> 00:05:02.718
Irate at losing Briseis, Achilles refused to fight, staying in his tent by the Greek ships and keeping his soldiers from the battle as well.

38
00:05:02.719 --> 00:05:10.298
In the following days, the war went poorly for the Greeks, and the Trojans battled them back from the city walls.

39
00:05:10.299 --> 00:05:22.647
Hoping to turn the tide in the Greeks' favor, Achilles' close friend Patroclus dressed himself in Achilles' armor and, carrying Achilles' sword and shield, led his soldiers into battle.

40
00:05:22.648 --> 00:05:29.218
Believing the mighty Achilles had returned, the Trojan soldiers fell back—except for one.

41
00:05:29.219 --> 00:05:33.258
Hector was Troy's great hero, and the brother of Paris.

42
00:05:33.259 --> 00:05:39.804
He boldly challenged Patroclus, thinking the young man in front of him was Achilles, and killed him.

43
00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:45.496
He stripped the corpse of Achilles' armor and weapons and triumphantly returned to Troy.

44
00:05:45.497 --> 00:05:49.210
Achilles vowed to take revenge for the death of his friend.

45
00:05:49.211 --> 00:05:54.179
He asked Hephaestus to craft a new set of armor and weapons for him.

46
00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:59.363
Then, Achilles sent a message to Hector that he had killed the wrong man.

47
00:05:59.364 --> 00:06:11.131
Hector knew that his chances of winning against the powerful Achilles and his enchanted armor were slim, but he dutifully dressed for battle once again and strode out of the city gates.

48
00:06:11.132 --> 00:06:18.194
The bloody clash between the two heroes left every soldier, Greek and Trojan alike, staring in awe.

49
00:06:18.195 --> 00:06:23.138
At last, Achilles killed Hector and avenged his slain friend.

50
00:06:23.139 --> 00:06:26.195
Achilles' grief, however, was undiminished.

51
00:06:26.196 --> 00:06:34.147
He fastened straps to Hector's body, attached them to his chariot, and dragged the fallen hero around the city walls.

52
00:06:34.148 --> 00:06:40.362
Priam, King of Troy and Hector's father, begged Achilles to stop, but to no avail.

53
00:06:40.363 --> 00:06:48.410
Finally, after Achilles had circled the city three times, he relented, and Priam took his son's remains away.

54
00:06:48.411 --> 00:06:54.735
Achilles then returned to the battlefield and fought heroically through the long years of the war.

55
00:06:54.736 --> 00:06:59.805
However, the gods Poseidon and Apollo had been watching the war closely.

56
00:06:59.806 --> 00:07:07.456
They supported opposite sides, but they were both infuriated by Achilles' arrogant desecration of Hector's body.

57
00:07:07.457 --> 00:07:15.495
They decided to intervene. Even though the Greeks went on to win the war, Achilles did not survive to see victory.

58
00:07:15.496 --> 00:07:19.495
Poseidon informed Paris of Achilles' unprotected heel.

59
00:07:19.496 --> 00:07:24.694
From the high walls of Troy, the prince searched for Achilles in the battle below.

60
00:07:24.695 --> 00:07:30.845
He fired an arrow at Achilles' heel, and Apollo guided the arrow to the vulnerable spot.

61
00:07:30.846 --> 00:07:37.894
Achilles fell to the ground, writhing in pain, and moments later, the great warrior was dead.

62
00:07:37.895 --> 00:07:44.308
Achilles' corpse was burned on a huge pyre and his ashes buried near his parents' home.

63
00:07:44.309 --> 00:07:52.562
Before his body was consumed in the flames, Thetis snatched her son's soul from the pyre and brought it to the island of Leuce.

64
00:07:52.563 --> 00:07:58.309
There, he rejoined his dead friends, including his beloved Patroclus.

